s/game (vs ~58); a rec-yards under lean.</p><h3>BUF \u2014 a <em>run-heavy</em> offense</h3><p>BUF are one of the league's more run-committed offenses: on early downs while <strong>leading</strong>, they pass just <strong>39%</strong> of the time, against a league average of 44% \u2014 a genuine clock-bleeding identity, not noise (it holds after shrinking their sample toward the league).</p><p><strong>James Cook</strong> (13 carries, 62 yards) projects to his baseline workload \u2014 no strong script push either way in a close game.</p><p><strong>Khalil Shakir</strong> projects for <strong>6 targets and 53 yards</strong>, in line with his established role in a script that neither inflates nor suppresses the pass.</p><p>Underneath, <strong>Dalton Kincaid</strong> (TE, 4 targets, 30 yards) is the dependable secondary read \u2014 and note the red zone stays TE-friendly regardless of score, so his touchdown equity holds up even if the game script shifts.</p><h3>NE \u2014 a <em>run-heavy</em> offense</h3><p>NE are one of the league's more run-committed offenses: on early downs while <strong>leading</strong>, they pass just <strong>39%</strong> of the time, against a league average of 44% \u2014 a genuine clock-bleeding identity, not noise (it holds after shrinking their sample toward the league).</p><p><strong>Rhamondre Stevenson</strong> (13 carries, 56 yards) projects to his baseline workload \u2014 no strong script push either way in a close game.</p><p><strong>A.J. Brown</strong> projects for <strong>7 targets and 69 yards</strong>, in line with his established role in a script that neither inflates nor suppresses the pass.</p><p>Underneath, <strong>Hunter Henry</strong> (TE, 4 targets, 36 yards) is the dependable secondary read \u2014 and note the red zone stays TE-friendly regardless of score, so his touchdown equity holds up even if the game script shifts.</p><h3>What to watch</h3><p>The single biggest swing factor is <strong>availability</strong> \u2014 use the i